    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s state from the expulsion of the kings in 509 BCE to Augustus in 27 BCE, ruled by elected magistrates, Senate and assemblies.</li><li>Its citizen army and Italian allies conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Hellenistic kingdoms, and took most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</li><li>From 133 BCE political violence and civil wars, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> to Caesar and Octavian, ended in monarchy under Augustus.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with little naval tradition, built fleets of a hundred or more warships at a time, and fitted them with a boarding bridge, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvus">corvus</a>, to win at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mylae">Mylae</a> in 260 BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

