    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus.jpg" alt="Lucius Junius Brutus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M._Junius_Brutus,_denarius,_54_BC,_RRC_433-2_(obverse_L._Junius).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to Roman tradition, the Republic began in 509 BCE, when aristocrats led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> expelled the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Lucius Tarquinius Superbus</a>, and replaced him with two magistrates elected each year, later called consuls. The traditional date is roughly confirmed by the treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> dated to the first year of the Republic, though the details of the story, including the rape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a>, are legend. The new state was a city of perhaps 25,000 to 40,000 people on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, already one of the largest in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In its first decades it fought the Etruscan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lars-porsenna">Lars Porsena</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cities at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Battle of Lake Regillus</a>, traditionally in 496 BCE, and in 493 BCE made an alliance with them, the <em>foedus Cassianum</em>. Wars with the Aequi, Volsci and the Etruscan city of Veii filled the fifth century, and Veii fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-furius-camillus">Marcus Furius Camillus</a> in 396 BCE, doubling Roman territory. Around 390 BCE, or 387 BCE in Greek reckoning, a Gallic war band defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-allia">Battle of the Allia</a> and sacked the city. Rome recovered quickly, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servian-wall">Servian Wall</a> of Grotta Oscura tufa and resumed its expansion in Latium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mediterranean.jpg" alt="Mediterranean" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">of the modification : Eric Gaba (Sting), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman society was divided at first between patricians, a closed group of aristocratic families, and plebeians, who in the Conflict of the Orders won the tribunate after the first secession of 494 BCE, written law in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> of 451–450 BCE, access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> from 366 BCE and binding force for plebeian resolutions under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-hortensia">Lex Hortensia</a> of 287 BCE. A new patricio-plebeian nobility of office-holding families emerged, bound to lesser citizens by patronage. The economy rested on farming by citizen smallholders, who also formed the army, but conquest transformed it. Booty, indemnities and provincial taxes flowed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and hundreds of thousands of captives were brought in as slaves, so that large estates worked by slave labour spread in parts of Italy alongside free farms, a process whose scale is debated. Slave revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 135 BCE and the rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a> in 73–71 BCE showed its dangers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a> was introduced around 211 BCE, and Roman and Italian traders, bankers and tax-farming companies, the <em>publicani</em>, spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Religion was a civic duty overseen by colleges of priests such as the pontiffs and augurs, and the state readily adopted foreign cults, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Great Mother</a>, Cybele, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 204 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae.jpg" alt="Battle of Cynoscephalae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_and_the_Aegean_World_c.200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kryston, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic’s army was a citizen militia levied each year from property-owning citizens and reinforced by contingents from Italian allies, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> deep reserves of manpower. In the fourth century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> gave way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manipular-legion">manipular</a> legion, flexible lines of maniples armed with the throwing spear, or <em>pilum</em>, and short sword. Rome defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> in 340–338 BCE, the Samnites in three wars ending in 290 BCE, and their allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sentinum">Battle of Sentinum</a> in 295 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> won costly victories at Heraclea in 280 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> in 279 BCE but withdrew after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> in 275 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> of 264–241 BCE Rome built fleets and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-aegates">Aegates Islands</a> in 241 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> destroyed Roman armies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> and Cannae in 218–216 BCE, but Rome refused terms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus</a> defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> in 202 BCE. The Macedonian phalanx fell at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Cynoscephalae</a> in 197 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Pydna</a> in 168 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE, and Carthage and Corinth were destroyed in 146 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> opened the legions to landless volunteers from 107 BCE, creating long-serving armies loyal to their commanders, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 58–50 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulla.jpg" alt="Sulla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Q._Pompeius_Rufus,_denarius,_54_BC,_RRC_434-1_(Sulla_only).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic had no written constitution but a body of laws and customs, the <em>mos maiorum</em>. Executive power, or <em>imperium</em>, belonged to annually elected magistrates: two consuls who led armies and presided over the state, praetors who handled justice and commands, and below them aediles and quaestors, with two censors chosen about every five years to register citizens and revise the Senate. In an emergency a dictator could be appointed for up to six months. Ten tribunes of the plebs could veto other magistrates and propose laws. The Senate, some 300 former magistrates serving for life and enlarged to about 600 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, had no formal legislative power, but its advice guided finance, foreign policy and the assignment of commands. Citizens voted in assemblies organised by wealth and tribe: the centuriate assembly elected senior magistrates and declared war, while the tribal assemblies elected lesser officials and passed most laws. Careers followed a fixed ladder of offices, the <em>cursus honorum</em>, regulated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-villia-annalis">Lex Villia Annalis</a> of 180 BCE. Conquered lands became provinces, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, taken in 241 BCE and given its own praetor in 227 BCE, governed by magistrates or promagistrates with few staff and little supervision, which encouraged extortion; a permanent court for recovering extorted money was created in 149 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> itself was bound by treaties with allied communities and by colonies of Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> citizens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odyssey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odyssey.jpg" alt="Odyssey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragment_Odyssee_2245_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic created the territorial empire that the emperors inherited, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> under one power and spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> across the western provinces, where it became the ancestor of the Romance languages. Its roads, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a> of 312 BCE, its colonies and its law shaped the map and institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, already developing through the edicts of praetors and the opinions of jurists, became the basis of later European legal systems. Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livius-andronicus">Livius Andronicus</a> translated the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a></em> into Latin around 240 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plautus">Plautus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terence">Terence</a> adapted Greek comedy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a> wrote the epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-ennius">Annales</a></em>, and in the late Republic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry. Caesar’s reform of the calendar in 46 BCE produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, the basis of the one used today. The Republic itself became a political model. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> explained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>’s success by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-constitution">mixed constitution</a> of monarchic, aristocratic and democratic elements, an idea taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> and the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, who borrowed the Senate, the veto and Roman pseudonyms such as Publius. Words such as republic, senate, consul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and dictator all come from its institutions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae.jpg" alt="Battle of Carrhae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Carrhae_(Caesar%CB%90_A_History_Of_The_Art_Of_War_Among_The_Romans_Down_To_The_End,_vol._II,_1892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Theodore Ayrault Dodge, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Conquest strained the Republic’s institutions. In 133 BCE the tribune <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> Sempronius Gracchus proposed to redistribute public land to poor citizens and was killed by senators; his brother Gaius met the same fate in 121 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91–88 BCE forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to grant citizenship to the Italian allies, and in 88 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a> marched his army on Rome, beginning civil wars that ended with his dictatorship and the proscriptions of 82–81 BCE. Extraordinary commands made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus</a> master of the East, where he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI</a>. In 60 BCE Pompey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> formed an informal alliance known as the First Triumvirate, but after Crassus died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a> in 53 BCE Caesar and Pompey fell out. Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, defeated Pompey at Pharsalus in 48 BCE and was made dictator for life early in 44 BCE, before senators led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Brutus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Cassius</a> murdered him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assassination-of-julius-caesar">Ides of March</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, Octavian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aemilius-lepidus-triumvir">Lepidus</a> defeated the assassins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Philippi</a> in 42 BCE. Octavian defeated Antony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Actium</a> in 31 BCE and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 30 BCE. In January 27 BCE he formally restored the Republic to the Senate and people and received the name Augustus, keeping real power and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principate">Principate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Roman Republic turned a city-state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> into the master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> through its citizen army and allied manpower, created institutions and law that shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and later republics, and destroyed itself in a century of civil wars that ended in the monarchy of Augustus.</em></p>

